### Discover, install and update

**Recommended: use the [Skills CLI](https://github.com/vercel-labs/skills)** with Node.js 22.20 or newer. Run these commands from the project where you want to use the skills:

```sh
# List the repository's available skills
npx skills add {{ repositoryURL .Specification }} --list

# Install every skill (to all supported agents, without prompts)
npx skills add {{ repositoryURL .Specification }} --all

# Install one skill and choose the target agent interactively
npx skills add {{ repositoryURL .Specification }} --skill {{ runSkillName .Specification.CommandLineCommands.Binary }}

# Install all skills to a specific agent instead
npx skills add {{ repositoryURL .Specification }} --skill '*' --agent codex

# Check and update previously installed skills
npx skills check
npx skills update
```

Installation is project-local by default; add `--global` for user-wide installation. To align with a software release, select its Git tag using the Skills CLI's `@<tag>` source syntax. Installation does not install the CLI binary itself.

Ask your agent to use the skill by name, or describe a matching task. Agent-specific setup and invocation are covered in the [skills guide](./guides/agent-skills.md#agent-specific-setup).

### Skills included in Linux packages

Package `{{ .Specification.PackageName }}` installs complete product skill directories under `{{ .Distribution.ToolSkillsPath }}/`. Their contents correspond to the packaged software version and include supporting scripts, references and assets. Package upgrades refresh these files; package removal removes them. They are ordinary package-owned files, readable by non-root users.

With the Skills CLI already available locally, no GitHub repository or marketplace is needed:

```sh
skills add {{ .Distribution.ToolSkillsPath }} --list
skills add {{ .Distribution.ToolSkillsPath }} --skill {{ runSkillName .Specification.CommandLineCommands.Binary }}
```

Alternatively, `npx skills add {{ .Distribution.ToolSkillsPath }} --all` uses the local skills, but npm may need network access to obtain the Skills CLI. CLI installs are managed copies; re-run the local installation after a package upgrade. Prefer the guide's direct links or directory configuration when you want agent skills to follow package upgrades automatically.

Linux packages never modify home directories or agent configuration. Activation is an explicit user choice. See [consuming packaged skills](./guides/agent-skills.md#consuming-packaged-skills) for offline linking and configuration.

### Generation and maintenance

Defining `command_line_commands` in the generator manifest enables product skills, this documentation, the skills guide and local QA tools. GitHub-hosted projects also receive the Agent Skills workflow. There is no separate skills switch. `no_actions: true` omits the GitHub Action integration skill but retains usage/install skills and their validation workflow. Projects without a CLI receive none of these outputs.

The [skills guide](./guides/agent-skills.md#validation-and-maintenance) explains local checks, authoring requirements and discovery through external platforms. The Git repository remains the canonical source.
{{ end -}}
